- Let $ d $ be the distance between Albatrocity and Finchester in miles. So, the round trip would be $ 2d $ miles in total.

- Let $ v $ be the pigeon's speed in still air (without any wind) in miles per hour. So, this is the speed the pigeon can maintain when there's no wind.

Then, when there is a wind blowing, the pigeon's effective speed will change depending on the direction of the trip. So, when the pigeon is flying from Albatrocity to Finchester, which is in the direction of the wind, the wind will assist the pigeon, so its effective speed will be $ v + w $, where $ w $ is the wind speed. Conversely, when the pigeon is flying back from Finchester to Albatrocity, against the wind, the effective speed will be $ v - w $, since the wind is now opposing the pigeon's flight.

In this problem, the wind speed $ w $ is given as 10 miles per hour, blowing from Albatrocity to Finchester. So, that means when the pigeon is flying from Albatrocity to Finchester, it has a tailwind, which adds to its speed, and when it's flying back, it has a headwind, which subtracts from its speed.

Now, I need to relate these speeds to the time it takes for the round trip under both conditions. Let me first consider the windless day scenario. On a windless day, the pigeon is flying both ways with the same speed, right? Because there's no wind, so both legs of the trip (from Albatrocity to Finchester and back) are at speed $ v $. So, the total time for the round trip would be the total distance divided by the speed. Since the total distance is $ 2d $, and the speed is $ v $, then the time taken would be:

$$
\text{Time}_{\text{windless}} = \frac{2d}{v}
$$

And we know that this time is 3 hours and 45 minutes, which is 3.75 hours. So, I can write that as an equation:

$$
\frac{2d}{v} = 3.75 \quad \text{(1)}
$$

Now, moving on to the case with wind. In this case, the pigeon is flying from Albatrocity to Finchester with the wind, so its speed is increased by the wind speed, which is 10 mph. So, going from Albatrocity to Finchester, the effective speed is $ v + 10 $ mph. Then, when it is flying back from Finchester to Albatrocity, it is flying against the wind, so its effective speed is decreased by the wind speed, which is $ v - 10 $ mph. 

